// Two lambdas read two different fields of one record through a filter-then-
// map chain. Each lambda's parameter is an OPEN row — `{op | r1}` for the
// filter's, `{body | r2}` for the map's — and by the time the map's meets the
// filter's result, the filter's row is already closed against the list. The
// rows must stay one row when they unify: the closing that reached one has to
// reach the other, and the body field sorts after the args field, so a row
// that forgot the closing reads args in body's place. Measured 2026-09-22 on
// the medium's own provider question, which died in exactly this shape with
// no diagnostic; this program answered 32 where 42 is right, in silence.
fn main() = {
  let arms = [{args: [1, 2, 3], body: 42, op: 7}]
  let found = arms |> filter({ a => a.op == 7 }) |> map({ a => a.body })
  found[0]
}
